{"id":"sand_pile","compound_name":"Graphs::MPM::sand_pile","source_path":"sand_pile/sand_pile.json","category":"Sand","author":"Autodesk","description":"A very simple grains simulation, in the form of a pile of sand.","documentation_file":"sand_pile_docs_ENU.md","documentation":"## Sand Pile Graph\n\n### Description\n\nThis graph shows how to use the MPM solver to simulate a pile of sand collapsing on the ground.\n\nThis graph takes a mesh as input to define the initial size, shape and location of the sand-pile, before it collapses.  By default, the graph creates a sphere to represent the initial sand shape, but the intention is that the user replace this with a mesh from the scene.\n\nSimilar to MPM snow, the mesh is then fed to a series of nodes that vary the properties of the MPM sand, such as cohesion, firmness, friction etc.  Also note that the sand gets its initial velocity from the `source_mpm_sand` node - look at the `Initial Speed` and `Direction` parameters.\n\nA mesh plane representing the ground is also constructed by default.  This is fed to the collider node so the MPM solver knows to collide the sand with it.  Again, this ground plane may be interchanged for other scene geometry that the sand should collide with.\n\nFinally, the sand is simulated via the `simulate_mpm` compound, a 'sand' material is assigned and the result is displayed in the viewport.\n\n### Inputs, Controls & Outputs\n\nThe main control for this graph is a \"sand pile\" mesh that controls the location, shape, and size of the initial sand.  There is also a mesh plane created for collision geometry (the floor).\n\nThe output is an MPM object containing the MPM particles.\n\nThe resolution controls for MPM simulations are similar to the Bifrost FLIP fluid solver in that it has both a 'particle density' control as well as a voxel size control.  The MPM method, like the FLIP method, is a hybrid method where the voxel size informs the resolution of the velocity field, whereas the particles-per-voxel control regulates the density of particles throughout the simulated body.\n\nResolution of the sand can be controlled via the following parameters:\n- `Particles Per Voxel` on the `source_mpm_sand` compound regulates the density of particles throughout the sand.  Increasing this parameter will add more particles to the sim, give higher spatial resolution, but slow down the solver a bit.  This also means the particle resolution is per-source, and not a global control.\n- `Detail Size` (found on the `mpm_solver_settings` compound) gives the size of the voxels used for computing velocities.  Decreasing this parameter will increase the detail of the motion of the sand, but at a higher computational cost than increasing the particle count (see above).\n\nAs with particle and aero graphs, you can connect arbitrary colliders and influence fields to the MPM solver.  Unlike Aero, however, the MPM solver is not adaptive, so there is only one setting for the voxel detail-size.\n\n### Best Practices and Tips\n\nThe following tips and tricks will be useful if you are getting into MPM simulation:\n\n- The best way to increase resolution without slowing down the sim too much is is to increase the `Particles Per Voxel` parameter on the `source_mpm_sand` compound.  This will give your sim more particles - greater detail - while not expanding the voxel grid.  Processing lots of particles is faster than processing large volumes.  Of course, at some point, you may need to lower the `detail_size`, but we recommend first seeing how far you get with lots of particles at a higher `detail_size`.\n- One of the great features of MPM is that the behavior of your sim doesn't radically change as you change the resolution (either particle and detail-size).  To become effective at MPM simulations you should embrace this feature by iterating at low resolutions (especially with large values for `detail_size`) until you get the look you want.  Only then, go for a high-res sim, knowing the look and behavior of the high-res sim will be similar to what you got at low-res.\n- We recommend caching your MPM sims as either .bob files (fastest, but only renderable at this time by Arnold), or as .vdb (slower, but immediately renderable by any renderer).  That said, you can still cache as .bob and then, once simming is complete, convert the .bob to .vdb by creating a simple graph that reads the .bob particles and writes them out as .vdb.  (And yes, VDB format does support particle data)\n- Note the `scene units to meters` port on the `mpm_solver_settings` compound.  This parameter allows you to model your scene in any scale you want - including centimeters - as long as you put the appropriate conversion in this parameter.  For example, if your scene is modeled in cm, then this parameter should be set to 0.01 (since 1cm = 0.01m).\n- For very slow sims, use the command-line solver called `bifcmd`.  This allows you to run slow, high-res sims on the command-line without hanging your Maya session with a heavy sim.  For more information about using `bifcmd`, see the main Bifrost documentation.  NOTE you will have to wrap your sim into a compound and publish it, and then run the `bifcmd` on that published compound.","library_dependencies":{"Amino":"2.15.0.9","bif":"2.15.0.9","fields":"2.15.0.9","geometries":"2.15.0.9","mpm":"2.15.0.9","scatter":"2.15.0.9","simulation":"2.15.0.9"},"graph_ports":[{"portName":"plane_mesh","portDirection":"output","portType":"Object"},{"portName":"out_geometry","portDirection":"output","portType":"array<Object>"}],"terminal_states":[{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::final","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::proxy","enabled":"false"},{"name":"Core::Graph::terminal::diagnostic","enabled":"false"}],"counts":{"nodes":37,"connections":42,"values":57,"compounds":5},"node_types":{"Core::Fields::scalar_field":3,"Rendering::Materials::assign_material":2,"Rendering::Materials::standard_surface_mat":2,"Core::Math::divide":2,"Core::Ma